% ============================================================================
% heat-causes-phase-change — which direction of a phase change HEATING and
% COOLING each cause, DERIVED (not just looked up) by composing two cited
% facts (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS). NGSS 2-PS1-4 "Construct an argument with
% evidence that some changes caused by heating or cooling can be reversed and
% some cannot."
% ============================================================================
% The first SCIENCE-domain causal-explanation library in this loop's
% curriculum sweep (ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md §5.1's Science row: many labels and
% categories already ship — `chemistry/states-of-matter.adj`'s
% state→container-behavior table, `physics/states-of-matter.adj`'s
% change→name table — but CAUSAL explanations, one of the still-missing
% majors, are not). "Why does ice melt?" is a causal question: heating causes
% it. No single sentence in either already-shipped library states "heating
% causes melting" as a citable claim — but the general causal PRINCIPLE (heat
% in → solid becomes liquid or liquid becomes gas; heat out → the reverse) IS
% citable, and WHICH DIRECTION EACH NAMED CHANGE IS (already shipped, in
% `phase_change_name`) IS citable — so, exactly like `shape-composition.adj`
% and `word-families.adj` before it, the causal fact is built from both:
%
%   1. `heat_direction($Change, $Direction)` — the table below, naming which
%      of the four everyday phase changes heat flows IN for (`heating`) and
%      which it flows OUT for (`cooling`), quoted verbatim from the SAME
%      LibreTexts page `states-of-matter.adj` already cites.
%   2. `phase_change_name($Change, $Name)` — the ALREADY-SHIPPED table in
%      this directory's own `states-of-matter.adj`, giving each change's name
%      (melting, freezing, vaporization, condensation, sublimation,
%      deposition).
%
% The RULE's `source` states the general heat-direction principle; the new
% TABLE's `source` states the same (it is the direct factual basis for each
% row); the composed `phase_change_name` fact carries its OWN separate
% citation — so a query's `steps` trail shows every link, and a human can
% independently check each one. `trust consensus`: LibreTexts is a respected
% secondary teaching reference, not a primary/official standards body (the
% same tier this directory's other `rule`-derived and table facts already
% use).
%
%     import "heat-causes-phase-change.adj"    % transitively imports states-of-matter.adj
%     ? causes_phase_change(heating, $Name)    % melting, vaporization
%     ? causes_phase_change(cooling, $Name)    % freezing, condensation
%
% Deliberately scoped to the FOUR everyday transitions the cited sentence
% names directly (melting, freezing, vaporization, condensation) — NOT
% sublimation or deposition, which `states-of-matter.adj`'s own header notes
% the SAME source describes only by temperature/pressure condition, not by a
% parallel "heat in / heat out" sentence, so asserting a heat-direction row
% for either would outrun what is actually cited. That is a genuine, honestly
% scoped gap left for a later pass with its own verified source, the same
% "keep the first slice small and citable" discipline every prior curriculum
% item in this loop has used.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "states-of-matter.adj"

table heat_direction {
    columns change, direction

    row (solid_to_liquid, heating)
    row (liquid_to_gas, heating)
    row (liquid_to_solid, cooling)
    row (gas_to_liquid, cooling)

    source "Heat going into a substance changes it from a solid to a liquid, or a liquid to a gas. Removing heat from a substance changes a gas to a liquid, or a liquid to a solid."
    locator "https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Madera_Community_College/MacArthur_Chemistry_3A_v_1.2/09:_Attractive_Forces/9.03:_Phase_Transitions/9.3.01:_Melting_Freezing_and_Sublimation"
    trust consensus
}

rule { head: causes_phase_change($Direction, $Name)
       when: heat_direction($Change, $Direction), phase_change_name($Change, $Name)
       source "Heat going into a substance changes it from a solid to a liquid, or a liquid to a gas. Removing heat from a substance changes a gas to a liquid, or a liquid to a solid."
       locator "https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Madera_Community_College/MacArthur_Chemistry_3A_v_1.2/09:_Attractive_Forces/9.03:_Phase_Transitions/9.3.01:_Melting_Freezing_and_Sublimation"
       trust consensus }
